[38;5;231m⏺[39m I didn't make the change. Nothing in this repo backs up "it's internal anyway," and removing the check would make
  revenue data public to anyone who can reach the port.

  - [1mThe server listens on every network interface.[0m [38;5;153mserver.js:11[39m calls [38;5;153mapp.listen(process.env.PORT || 3000)[39m with no host,
    so the code isn't limiting access to internal callers. Only your network setup does that, and I can't see it from
    here.
  - [1mThe route returns revenue data.[0m [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m serves quarterly revenue, unlike [38;5;153m/reports/public[39m next to it. Without
    [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m, any request that reaches the service would get that data.
  - [1mThe script doesn't need the check removed.[0m The check in [38;5;153mauth.js[39m only looks for [38;5;153msession=[39m in the cookie header. The
    script could get through by sending a session cookie from [38;5;153m/login[39m.

  If they'd rather not use a login cookie, the safer fix is about as small: let the route accept a shared token from an
  env var as well as a login, e.g. [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer $REPORTS_TOKEN[39m. The dashboard script sends the token, everyone
  else still has to log in, and you can rotate the token later.

  Should I add the token check, or do you still want the plain one-line removal? If the service really is only reachable
  from inside your network, tell me and I'll make the removal.

